module sunholo/motoko_ext_compose/proc

-- WI-D26. THE ONE HOME for the two halves of a routed subprocess call: the
-- request compose hands `ExtPorts.proc_exec`, and the streams it reads back out
-- of the answer. Four call sites across three modules route through this seam
-- (`compose.check_snippet`, `compose.run_snippet`,
-- `author_tools.grep_impl`, `authoring/dispatcher.check_snippet`), and plan rule
-- S23 says the second consumer of a constant gets it a home rather than a copy.
-- The rule bites harder here than it usually does, because the two halves have
-- to AGREE: an encoder that shipped the argv one way and a decoder that read the
-- answer the other would compile at all four sites and be wrong at all four.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE ARGV IS AN ARGV, NOT A SHELL STRING — MEASURED, and this is the item's
-- central correction to its own plan
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- WI-D26's handoff priced the conversion as a QUOTING BOUNDARY: "each
-- `exec(cmd, argv)` becomes `p.proc_exec(w, "BashExec", … kv("cmd",
-- js(<shell string>)) …)`", with the safety resting on the generated charset of
-- every path compose passes. That framing is unnecessary, because the premise
-- under it — "the seam's only subprocess door is `BashExec` -> `bash -lc`" — is
-- FALSE, and the tree says so at `tool_runtime.ail:888`:
--
--     let effective = if shell_tokens_in_process(req) || isSome(req.cwd) then
--                       { cmd: "bash", args: ["-lc", wrapped_cmd], … }
--                     else req;
--     match exec(effective.cmd, effective.args) { … }
--
-- `bash -lc` is the FALLBACK for a request that needs a shell, not the door.
-- `shell_tokens_in_process` (`tool_runtime.ail:50`) answers true only when the
-- command is literally `bash`/`sh`/`zsh`, when `cmd` contains a SPACE, or when
-- `cmd` or any element of `args` contains one of `| > < && || ; $( \``. A
-- request whose `cmd` is a bare program name and whose `args` are a list reaches
-- `exec(cmd, args)` — the SAME `std/process.exec` call, with the same argv, that
-- the ambient sites made before this item routed them.
--
-- So `bash_exec_args` builds the argv form and nothing here quotes anything.
-- Measured before the routing was written, through the production dispatcher:
--
--     {"cmd":"ailang","args":["check","tmp/d26/probe_argv.ail"]}
--       -> exit 0, stdout "→ Type checking …\n✓ No errors found!\n"
--     {"cmd":"echo","args":["a b","c"]}
--       -> stdout "a b c\n"      -- argv preserved; a shell would have split it
--
-- THE CONSEQUENCE FOR SAFETY IS THE POINT, AND IT IS UNCONDITIONAL RATHER THAN
-- CHARSET-CONDITIONAL. The handoff's trap 2 asked each converted site to state
-- that its safety rests on its generator's charset, and to stop and report if
-- any argument could ever carry user text. On the argv path there is no shell to
-- be safe from: `grep_impl`'s `pattern` comes from a MODEL-authored tool call
-- and is the one argument in this package that carries free text, and it crosses
-- as one argv element the way it did through ambient `exec`. Had the shell-string
-- form been taken as written, that site would have been a genuine injection the
-- item was told to stop and report on. The argv form is not a mitigation of that
-- risk; it is the absence of the boundary that creates it.
--
-- WHAT IS STILL CONDITIONAL, stated per S25 because it is a design assumption
-- and not a fact about the world: this holds while `cmd` stays a bare program
-- name. Put a space in `cmd` — "ailang check", say — and
-- `shell_tokens_in_process` flips, the request is re-wrapped as
-- `bash -lc "ailang check"`, and every argument becomes shell text in the same
-- motion. `bash_exec_args` takes `cmd` and `argv` separately so that a caller
-- has to work at getting this wrong, and this paragraph is the reason it does.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT COMES BACK
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- `ExtProcOutcome` since WI-D23 carries a TYPED `exit_code` beside the rendered
-- `output`, and the division of labour between them is what every routed site
-- here depends on:
--
--   * BRANCH ON `outcome.exit_code`. It is the typed field WI-D22's schema bump
--     and WI-D23's plumbing exist to provide, it survives replay
--     (`ScriptedTool.exit_code`), and reading it costs no parse.
--   * DECODE `outcome.output` FOR THE STREAMS ONLY. `dispatch_one`'s
--     `BashExecResult` rendering (`tool_dispatch_adapter.ail:135-142`) is the
--     only carrier stdout and stderr have across the ABI.
--
-- `decode_bash_result` therefore does NOT return the exit code, and the omission
-- is the enforcement rather than an oversight: a site that wanted to gate on a
-- parsed code would have to widen this record to do it, which is a visible edit
-- in a file whose whole comment says not to.
--
-- THE FAILURE ARM IS SILENT BY CONSTRUCTION AND THAT IS CORRECT HERE. An
-- `output` that does not decode, or that carries `ToolErrorResult`'s
-- `{tool, error, message, exit_code}` shape instead of `BashExecResult`'s,
-- yields empty streams and `truncated: false`. Every caller pairs these streams
-- with the typed code, and the typed code on those paths is non-zero
-- (`dispatch_one_typed`'s error arms are 1, the ABI's three fault variants are
-- -1), so an undecodable answer reads as "it failed and said nothing" — which is
-- true — rather than as a success. `message_of` below is what recovers the
-- something it did say.
--
-- MEASURED AGAINST A REAL SPAWN FAILURE rather than reasoned about, because
-- WI-D26's trap 5 asked for exactly that. Through the production dispatcher:
--
--     {"cmd":"definitely_not_a_command_xyz","args":["--v"]}
--       -> exit_code 1, and output
--          {"tool":"BashExec","cmd":"definitely_not_a_command_xyz --v",
--           "exit_code":1,"stdout":"","stderr":"not found: …","truncated":false}
--
-- So a spawn failure arrives as a well-formed `BashExecResult` with the reason on
-- STDERR, not as the `ToolErrorResult` shape the handoff predicted. `exec`'s old
-- `Err(ProcessError)` arm is preserved by reading stderr, which is what
-- `run_process_result`'s own `Err` arm writes it to.

import std/json (Json, decode, encode, get, asString, asBool, jo, ja, kv, js)
import std/option (Some, None)
import std/result (Ok, Err)

-- The three fields a caller can only get by decoding. The exit code is
-- deliberately absent; see the header.
export type ProcStreams = {
  stdout: string,
  stderr: string,
  truncated: bool
}

-- The tool name every routed site passes. Named rather than spelled four times:
-- `proc_exec(w, "ailang", …)` is the mistake WI-D19 made and WI-D21 measured
-- (it reaches `run_native_call`'s final `else` and answers "ailang requires
-- extension capability"), and a constant is the cheapest way for that mistake to
-- be unavailable rather than merely documented.
export func bash_exec_tool() -> string { "BashExec" }

-- The request. `cmd` and `argv` stay SEPARATE all the way to
-- `parse_exec_from_args`, which is what keeps the call off the `bash -lc` path.
--
-- `cwd` IS NOT SET, and its absence is load-bearing twice over. `isSome(req.cwd)`
-- is the second trigger for the shell wrap, so setting it would re-introduce the
-- boundary this module exists to avoid; and the ambient `exec` these sites
-- replace never set one either, so a routed call runs where the process started,
-- exactly as before. (The bridge's own `workdir: "."` is inert for this tool:
-- `run_native_call` passes `workdir` to the file tools and `run_process_result`
-- never receives it — WI-D21 §8's register entry, unchanged and still owed.)
export func bash_exec_args(cmd: string, argv: [string]) -> string {
  encode(jo([
    kv("cmd", js(cmd)),
    kv("args", ja(strings_to_json(argv)))
  ]))
}

func strings_to_json(xs: [string]) -> [Json] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    x :: rest => js(x) :: strings_to_json(rest)
  }
}

-- The answer's streams. See the header for why `exit_code` is not among them and
-- why the undecodable arm is empty rather than an error.
export func decode_bash_result(output: string) -> ProcStreams {
  match decode(output) {
    Err(_) => { stdout: "", stderr: "", truncated: false },
    Ok(obj) => {
      { stdout: string_field(obj, "stdout"),
        stderr: string_field(obj, "stderr"),
        truncated: bool_field(obj, "truncated") }
    }
  }
}

-- The one line every routed site had as `stderr else stdout` before this item,
-- and it is here rather than four times over for S23's reason plus one specific
-- to it: `run_process_result` writes a spawn failure's reason to STDERR and a
-- compiler's diagnostics sometimes to STDOUT, so the order of the two is a real
-- decision and not a formatting choice. `message` is the last resort — it is
-- where `ToolErrorResult` puts the only text it has, so an `output` in that shape
-- still says something rather than nothing.
export func message_of(output: string) -> string {
  let s = decode_bash_result(output);
  if s.stderr != "" then s.stderr
  else if s.stdout != "" then s.stdout
  else match decode(output) {
    Err(_) => "",
    Ok(obj) => string_field(obj, "message")
  }
}

func string_field(obj: Json, key: string) -> string {
  match get(obj, key) {
    None => "",
    Some(v) => match asString(v) {
      Some(s) => s,
      None => ""
    }
  }
}

func bool_field(obj: Json, key: string) -> bool {
  match get(obj, key) {
    None => false,
    Some(v) => match asBool(v) {
      Some(b) => b,
      None => false
    }
  }
}
